  "text": "Dáire is an Irish hero's name. The name appears often in Irish mythology. It means fertile or fruitful. Writers shape it into English as Dara. The name Dara may come from doire, which means oak or oak grove. Some scholars say the many figures with this name share one root. The root may be a god who ruled fertility, a god like the Dagda. Several major figures carry the name. One Dáire was the father of Lugaidh. This father named all four of his sons Lugaidh. Another Dáire was the son of Fionn Mac Cumhaill. Dáire of the Poems was a bard and musician of the Fianna. Dáire Derg was a red one-eyed villain. His children, the Fothads, fought Fionn Mac Cumhaill. Dáire mac Dedad was the ancestor of the Érainn people. Dáire mac Fiachan owned the bull Donn. His refusal to give the bull to Queen Medb began the cattle raid in Connaught.",
